well there are a lot of great things  happening here in st. Louis the chess  campus as we call it so many different  activities a US Championship to be sure  top level games but a lot of youngster  young activities that is for the youth  here in st. Louis and Bryan caseta who  is a professor at the University of  Missouri is part of the team doing some  research on all the great scholastic  efforts that are being done here Brian  welcome to the show tell us what you do  right now in Missouri well I'm a  professor at the University of Missouri  I study education policy so I'm in the  Department of Economics in the School of  Public Affairs there and specifically we  look at education policies my specific  research agenda I'm really interested in  things that engage students and make  students give them opportunities that  they don't necessarily have this is  ranged from studying the effects of arts  education education q manatees after  school programs field trip programs so  working with the Chess Club is a natural  fit because this is a-you know the  scholastic program here in st. Louis is  a perfect example of the type of program  that can bring students something that  they can't access in any other way now  you mentioned that you've done all these  different sorts of activities why is  chess such a natural match for the kind  of things that you're doing well I think  one of the most fascinating things about  chess is that it really seems to hit two  specific areas that we care a lot about  one is the academic component I mean  chess naturally translates to things  like spatial reasoning and math skills  there's been some studies that have come  out of Europe that show that students  who play chess are better at math their  math scores go up particularly study in  Italy study in Denmark and that's  obviously something that we care about  but that's not the only thing we care  about we also want students to be  engaged at school we want them to be  intrinsically motivated to learn and to  take on challenges and to build their  confidence chess has that component as  well if you can get students to have fun  while they're learning you've you've  really figured out a way to you know  crack that nut and move the needle now a  lot of people want to see numbers they  want to see that there are concrete  results not just anecdotal evidence to  prove that chess is great for kids even  though we all know chess is fantastic  for course but what has a the number  said you're the research guy what have  you seen to be just really credible  numbers that suggests that it's chess  that we should be incorporating into our  nation's schools  well there have not been you know any  big large-scale research studies in the  United States to date there's the  European studies where you know there  are you know countries in Europe that  shows where chess is part of the  curriculum those studies have mainly  focused on academic and achievement  effects and they've found like I said  typically increases in math achievement  the research that we've done so far to  date and st. Louis and in you know some  of the schools in Ferguson is we're  looking at the more of the student  engagement questions and we've piloted  surveys with the students that are in  the clubs with some pretty you know  overwhelming you know feedback that  we're getting from these students in  terms of the way that they view the Tres  program so for example we've we've had  the the feedback surveys have shown that  more than 90 percent of these students  agree that chess makes them more  confident that they can accomplish  difficult difficult tasks makes them  want to challenge themselves more in the  future and I think probably the one that  you know gives us some of the most  confidence is 85% of the students in  these programs say that they look  forward to coming to school more on days  when they have chess and that's huge  because getting getting kids to school  is is you know sometimes one of the  hardest things that's right in chess day  for my students is usually a key day  they want to get out of bed it's like  okay we're gonna be playing chess with  mr. Ashley today so they're usually  pretty happy about that now we're  working on a project together along with  other members of the team dr. Matthew  pepper Tony and Kareem both Tony's a  bunch of Tony Chen and Richard pointer  and we are having a great time we're  putting together this curriculum tell  the folks some details about what we're  doing in this effort right so we know  when you get involved with research  there's really you know two important  points here one is certainly we want to  know what the impact is we want to know  what the outcome is but implementation  is huge you know there was a big study  in England recently that did not find  positive effects from  but that study came out and reported  that implementation may have been the  problem in that maybe the teachers  weren't prepped properly they didn't  have enough resources and materials so  we're focused a lot right now with you  on designing making sure that  implementation is right in four key  areas so we're devising a set of  standards that are going to be  competency-based that sequentially sort  of walk students through you know the  beginning day of you know how to set up  a chessboard all the way to eventually  being a grandmaster like yourself we're  working on curricular materials that  will be attached to those standards so  the instructors here at the Chess Club  have the tools that they need to engage  those students we're looking at a lot of  digital options things that students can  use on tablets and their phones we're  gonna build in a framework for  assessments so the instructors can  monitor the students progress know where  the gaps are know where they need to  target their efforts and then a final  component is working really on the  certification of the chess instructors  to make sure that before they go out  into the classroom they have everything  that they need and they're ready to go  that is so thorough you studied so  perfectly and it's going to be an  amazing effort I can't wait and for us  to finish this job and release it to the  world and everybody see what we're doing  here and say Lois Brian thanks so much  for being a colleague look forward to  doing some more work with you thank you  very frank casita at the University of  Missouri helping the transform chess not  just here in st. Louis but around the  country
 

